  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1108 AM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nA 1025 mb sfc high pressure over the central Plains will begin to \nbuild in tonight with cold tranquil weather conditions. Skies will \nquickly clear out by this evening as drier air filters in, favoring \nrobust radiational cooling overnight. Widespread frost is probable \nover most of the FA with areas of freeze expected across the \nnorthwest where the boundary layer will decouple the greatest. \nBoth the CONSShort and MOS guidance are running slightly warmer \nthan the NBM, especially across the eastern half where there \nwill still be a subtle pressure gradient to keep winds at night \naround 3-4 kts. Given a shallow temperature inversion that is \nabout 1C warmer around 500 ft, have not made any adjustments to \nthe Frost and Freeze headlines for Saturday morning as any \nadditional locations that reach 32 degrees will be very brief. \nWith that said, a Freeze Watch remains in effect for the entire \nregion Saturday night as the growing season will likely come to \nan end Sunday morning. \n\nThe chilly airmass will not last for long as temperatures trend \nabove normal on Monday to start the week. Highs will rise into \nthe upper 60s to near 70 degrees through Wednesday. Mild \nconditions will not last long as a more amplified trough is \nprogged to dig into the lower Ohio Valley during the latter half\nof the week. In the wake of 850 mb temperatures plummeting \nbelow 0C, a cold front sweeps across the FA Wednesday afternoon \ninto Wednesday evening. While no rain is progged by the NBM, the\nLREF would support a 20-30% chance of very light anafront \nprecipitation Wednesday evening into Wednesday night. Would also\nnot be surprised to see additional sprinkles or light rain \nshower chances sneak in to end the week as a potent vort max \nwill be centered over the FA with a prevailing northwest flow \nregime.",
